1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,599
Meet Harness.

2
00:00:01,699 --> 00:00:09,071
A lightweight, local-first MCP server, written in Go, that gives your AI coding agents your engineering experience.

3
00:00:10,121 --> 00:00:13,786
Most AI coding assistants start from zero on every prompt.

4
00:00:13,886 --> 00:00:18,114
They don't know the race condition you fixed in your ledger service six months ago.

5
00:00:18,214 --> 00:00:23,522
They don't know your team's rules, like requiring idempotency keys on payment webhooks.

6
00:00:23,622 --> 00:00:28,853
So they fall back to generic boilerplate that breaks constraints you've already learned the hard way.

7
00:00:29,903 --> 00:00:32,910
Harness fills that gap with a persistent memory layer.

8
00:00:33,010 --> 00:00:38,323
Dev logs record the problems you solved, how you solved them, and the trade-offs you accepted.

9
00:00:38,423 --> 00:00:44,111
Architectural profiles hold each project's style, stack, and strict do and don't rules.

10
00:00:44,211 --> 00:00:47,961
Incident post-mortems capture root causes and prevention rules.

11
00:00:48,061 --> 00:00:51,712
Feature contexts track the invariants that must never break.

12
00:00:51,812 --> 00:00:56,409
And code exemplars store your golden, approved snippets by language and topic.

13
00:00:57,459 --> 00:00:59,356
The workflow is a simple loop.

14
00:00:59,456 --> 00:01:03,012
Capture: you, or your agent, record what happened.

15
00:01:03,112 --> 00:01:09,788
Index: Harness stores it in SQLite, with FTS5 full-text search kept in sync by triggers.

16
00:01:09,888 --> 00:01:17,597
Inject: before writing code, the agent calls Harness over MCP to pull in the relevant history, rules, and examples.

17
00:01:18,647 --> 00:01:22,048
Under the hood, Harness is a single static Go binary.

18
00:01:22,148 --> 00:01:27,616
Agents connect over stdio, or over Streamable HTTP for a shared server.

19
00:01:27,716 --> 00:01:32,760
Fifteen tools sit on top of a type-safe repository generated with sqlc.

20
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:38,920
Everything lands in an embedded SQLite database in WAL mode, using a pure-Go driver with no CGO.

21
00:01:39,970 --> 00:01:42,067
Getting started takes about a minute.

22
00:01:42,167 --> 00:01:45,443
Clone the repo and install the pinned goose migration tool.

23
00:01:45,543 --> 00:01:48,411
Apply the migrations to create the database.

24
00:01:48,511 --> 00:01:49,976
Then build the binary.

25
00:01:50,076 --> 00:01:55,763
Run it, and Harness connects to SQLite and starts serving MCP on port 9090.

26
00:01:56,813 --> 00:01:59,064
Next, connect your agents.

27
00:01:59,164 --> 00:02:04,507
Claude Code needs a single command; other clients need just a few lines of JSON config.

28
00:02:04,607 --> 00:02:11,979
It works with Claude, Antigravity, Gemini CLI, Cursor, VS Code, Windsurf, and Codex.

29
00:02:12,079 --> 00:02:17,502
Point them all at the same database, and a fix recorded in one agent is available to every other.

30
00:02:18,552 --> 00:02:20,827
Here's a real session against the server.

31
00:02:20,927 --> 00:02:23,274
The agent discovers fifteen tools.

32
00:02:23,374 --> 00:02:31,385
After fixing a bug where webhook retries double-credited wallets, it records a dev log with the problem, the solution, and the trade-offs.

33
00:02:31,485 --> 00:02:38,538
Weeks later, a different agent searches for webhook idempotency, and FTS5 ranks that exact fix at the top.

34
00:02:39,588 --> 00:02:45,652
To make it automatic, add a short memory protocol to your CLAUDE.md or GEMINI.md.

35
00:02:45,752 --> 00:02:52,044
Now, when you ask for idempotency on the payment webhook, the agent first loads your architecture rules,

36
00:02:52,144 --> 00:02:56,794
searches past fixes and incidents, and pulls in your golden code exemplars.

37
00:02:56,894 --> 00:03:02,061
It writes code the way you would, then records a new dev log, so the memory keeps growing.

38
00:03:03,111 --> 00:03:06,734
Harness. Your engineering experience, in every agent.

39
00:03:06,834 --> 00:03:12,917
It's open source under the MIT license and in early development, so contributions are welcome.

40
00:03:13,017 --> 00:03:16,912
Find it on GitHub at Ayobami6 slash harness.
